Socket lifetime proposal25 — design only; independent review required

Recommendation
Use a nonblocking listening descriptor with finite readiness waits and retain its ownership until every accept/readiness borrower has returned. Add the same scoped descriptor ownership to connected-socket I/O and timeout changes, with shutdown protected by a close/control borrower. Retire admission atomically; physically close a descriptor exactly once, only after no borrower can issue another syscall using its number. Preserve blocking accepted sockets, existing receive/send timeout meanings, public APIs, request deadlines, budgets, test selection and suite concurrency. Do not implement or execute this proposal until the coordinator assigns a writer after independent design review.

Evidence boundary
The supplied diagnostic23 sample contains testUpgradeRequestInvalidConnection → JPIPWebSocketServer.stop → JPIPServer.stop → JPIPTCPListener.close → posixCloseSocket → close on a cooperative executor, alongside accept queues labelled8888 and9096 in __accept. The sample does not contain enough descriptor/lock/kernel identity to prove which accept is coupled to that close, prove an FD was reused, establish a kernel bug, or explain the earlier HTTPS failures. Root reports a >6-minute XCTest stall, intervention by SIGINT of its own XCTest process, later Swift Testing continuation and overall FAIL. This proposal did not reconcile the complete mixed run; that account is supplied context, not new acceptance evidence.

The source risk is independently visible: listener accept snapshots fd under NSLock and releases it before accept; close swaps fd to-1 and calls shutdown/close without an in-flight ownership record. A close between snapshot and syscall permits the raw integer to be reused; EINTR retry can repeat that use. Locking the property alone does not protect the syscall's lifetime. The same pattern exists in connected-socket recv/send and timeout setters. These are source-supported races to repair, not demonstrated explanations for the sampled stall.

Small implementation scope
1. JPIPTCPSocket.swift: a small private descriptor-lifetime state/helper shared by the listener and connection, or equivalent clearly identical scoped ownership in each class; listener nonblocking setup/poll loop and accepted-descriptor publication; all connection descriptor consumers; corrected concurrency comments. Separate shutdown from physical close instead of retaining one helper that performs both indiscriminately. Keep unexposed construction/failed-connect cleanup exclusive and simple.
2. JPIPServer.swift: only capture the retiring listener and observe its descriptor-close completion asynchronously at the existing async stop boundary. Retire/close the socket pool before this wait, preserve late pool rejection, and perform no new shared-state cleanup after that wait. No new full-server-worker join is silently added.
3. New focused tests for descriptor ownership, accept publication, cancellation and error paths. Existing timing assertions, setup defaults, deadline origins, parallel execution and service-class settings stay unchanged. Do not rewrite transport policies, the parser, URLSession/HTTPS, shared contracts, dependencies or public method signatures.
4. Immediate transport callers require audit against the new close boundary; their generation and owning-result publication logic should remain unchanged unless the writer identifies a concrete missing close-completion dependency for separate review.

Descriptor state and invariants
Use one owner per newly created descriptor, never a reopened/reassigned fd inside the same owner. State under one short lock includes admission-open/retired, the owned fd, active borrower count, close/control status, final-close claimed/finished and completion waiters. The raw integer is obtainable only inside a scoped synchronous borrow. Acquiring a borrow and checking admission are one locked operation. Borrow release runs in defer on every success/error/early-return path. The borrow retains the lifetime object strongly; no raw fd or borrowed closure escapes into another task.

The first close atomically retires admission. isOpen becomes false immediately. All later close calls are idempotent and cannot obtain a second raw close/shutdown right. Existing borrowers still retain the original descriptor; no physical close or allocator reuse is permitted while any borrower or required control action exists. The last eligible release claims the unique physical-close action under the state lock, takes the fd out of state, unlocks, performs close, then publishes its terminal result and resumes completion waiters outside the lock. With no borrowers, first close can claim the same action directly. No state/read/write lock is held during accept, poll, recv, send, physical close, callbacks or waiter resumption.

Completion must distinguish logical retirement from physical-close completion. An async package/private wait may reuse the existing broadcast-completion pattern, but it must not mark success before the final close call returns. All concurrent waiters observe the same terminal result. Preserve an error result for a failed close attempt; do not represent an error as proven descriptor release. Never blindly retry close after EINTR or another failure, because that can target a recycled number. Review Darwin/Glibc error policy explicitly; a terminal error must remain observable in tests and in any caller claiming successful close completion. No universal kernel-time or physical-thread-exit guarantee follows.

Listener algorithm
A. During exclusive listener construction, obtain current flags with F_GETFL, set O_NONBLOCK using F_SETFL while preserving other flags, and check both results. Do this before publishing the listener. Any failure closes the exclusive descriptor exactly once and throws the existing network-error category. Keep bind/listen/backlog/host/port behavior unchanged.
B. acceptConnection acquires one descriptor borrower for its complete wait/accept/publication scope. Closed-before-acquisition returns nil. It still blocks only its existing dedicated OS thread/Dispatch queue, never a cooperative executor. timeoutSeconds continues to configure the ACCEPTED socket only; it is not a listener idle deadline.
C. Check retired state before every wait/accept/retry. Use poll with POLLIN and a small finite constant wait quantum (suggest50ms, subject to design review). Timeout returns to the closed-state check and repeats while open; it does not end acceptConnection. No sleeps or infinite poll timeout. Readiness can become stale, so the actual accept syscall must use the already-nonblocking listener. EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK repeat the readiness loop; EINTR first checks closed state. A pending-connection ECONNABORTED is a failed connection, not proof the listener closed; retry via the readiness loop. Unexpected poll/accept errors surface without a busy internal spin. Treat POLLNVAL while owner-open as an invariant/error, not evidence that another close was safe. POLLERR/HUP require explicit handling, and a closed owner wins before another accept attempt.
D. Holding the borrower across poll and accept prevents FD reuse even if close occurs just after a closed-state check. On close, the listener does NOT call shutdown to interrupt accept and does NOT physically close under an active borrower. The finite wait plus nonblocking accept lets the worker observe retirement and release its borrower. A nominal poll quantum is a maximum requested kernel wait, not a guaranteed end-to-end stop latency under scheduling delay.
E. A successful accept produces a new exclusively owned raw clientFD. Before constructing/publishing a JPIPTCPConnection, explicitly clear O_NONBLOCK with checked F_GETFL/F_SETFL, preserving all other flags. This is necessary on both platforms; never rely on inheritance or noninheritance. Only then apply the unchanged connected-socket timeout/TCP_NODELAY/SIGPIPE setup. If setup fails or listener retirement wins before publication, close the unexposed accepted descriptor once and return nil for retirement or throw the setup error; do not leak it.
F. Recheck listener retirement under the same lifetime lock at the publication/ownership-transfer point. Close-before-this-point rejects the late accepted socket. If publication wins first, ownership transfers to the caller; a concurrent close can occur before that caller is scheduled to receive the result. This is the precise linearizable contract, not an impossible promise that no concurrently successful call can return after close's wall-clock return. The server's stopped socket-pool admission must reject/close this already-transferred late arrival. The listener must never later close a transferred connection.

Connected-socket inventory and repair
The current consumers requiring a protected borrow are:
- setReceiveTimeout(interval:) around setsockopt, lines596–603.
- setSendTimeout(interval:) around setsockopt, lines605–612.
- writeAll, currentFD line653 and send loop658.
- fillOnceLocked, currentFD687 and recv691.
- readExactLocked, currentFD733 and recv738.
- readToEOF, currentFD757 and both recv sites762/771.
- readWebSocketExact, currentFD1412 and recv1435.
Constructors configure an exclusive not-yet-published descriptor, so their setup is not a shared snapshot. Search the final source for every raw fd syscall again; currentFD must not remain as an unleased escape hatch.

Use one scoped borrow for each existing raw I/O loop, not a lock held across blocking I/O. Keep readLock/wsReadLock/writeLock and full-duplex behavior as currently designed; lock order is operation lock → short descriptor-state access, and close never acquires an operation lock. Timeout setters use a short borrow and remain no-op when already logically closed. Retain socket blocking mode, existing SO_RCVTIMEO/SO_SNDTIMEO values, zero-timeout semantics, allocation admission, framing and error categories.

For connection close, atomically retire admission and obtain one control borrower while the fd is still owned. Perform shutdown(SHUT_RDWR) on that retained descriptor outside the state lock. Release the control borrower only after shutdown returns. This prevents an I/O borrower from finishing and physically closing/reusing the fd before shutdown reaches it. Physical close is possible only after the control borrower AND all I/O/setsockopt borrowers have returned. Other close calls do not repeat shutdown or close. Completion waiters must wait for this sequence, not merely observe fd=-1.

Check logical retirement at EINTR retries and before additional loop syscalls so a cancelled partial operation does not start a new iteration. A syscall already admitted before retirement can complete; caller generation/cancellation checks remain responsible for rejecting retired results. Do not retroactively redefine successful complete reads or copy semantics. Connected shutdown preserves the existing cancellation mechanism; test that blocked receive/send returns on supported Darwin/Glibc runtimes. The design does not infer a portable listener-accept wakeup from connected shutdown behavior. A platform failing connected cancellation remains an open validation failure, not permission to close a borrowed fd early.

Connecting-token boundary
JPIPSocketCancellationToken already guards connectingFD, clears it before failed-connect physical close, and executes connecting shutdown under its own lock. Keep that ownership protocol. The connect worker exclusively owns creation/configuration/connect; completeConnect clears the raw connecting slot and transfers to the connection owner under the token lock. Do not add a second competing raw descriptor owner, duplicate close, or cancellation closure storing an unleased fd. The activeConnection branch calls the repaired connection.close outside the token lock. DNS and blocking connect latency remain existing qualifications; no new guarantee or connect redesign is claimed.

Caller completion obligations and scope edge
JPIPServer.start currently uses an accept dispatch queue and concurrent client queue. stop retires the listener/pool but does not join those queues. Its synchronous listener close occurs on the actor executor in the supplied sample. Under this repair, an in-flight accept/poll borrower prevents that stop call from physically closing the same descriptor; the accept worker performs final close after it returns from its finite wait. Add asynchronous observation of that captured listener's final-close completion so stop does not report listener release/rebind readiness merely from isOpen=false. Close the old pool before suspension, capture only the old listener, and put the added wait after existing actor-state cleanup to avoid mutating a new generation after this added await. Never block the actor with NSCondition, semaphore/group wait, or polling for completion. A close with zero active borrowers has no concurrent accept to join, but still carries no universal kernel-latency promise.

Do NOT silently await every accept/client queue in this small patch. The maxClients=503 branch writes before inserting its connection into the pool, and client workers can await actor work through existing semaphore bridges. A new full accept-loop join would require tracking that pending connection and proving actor/restart ordering, beyond just fixing the descriptor race. The pool already closes insertions after stopped, covering the publication-wins-before-stop case. Server stop's added receipt means captured LISTENER descriptor closure, not all server tasks, all connection descriptors or physical thread exit. If full stop quiescence is required, define that as a separately reviewed extension with tracked pre-admission sockets; do not claim it here.

For HTTP transport, JPIPBlockingWorker publishes the result only after the synchronous operation returns, then signals JPIPHTTPRequestOwner.workerFinished. Every descriptor borrow must have been released before that callback; cancellation shutdown must retain its own control borrower. Existing transport close joins captured request owners and keeps generation checks. WebSocket disconnect closes sockets but terminal reader/actor callbacks can finish later; keep that documented boundary. Connection-close completion tests should explicitly distinguish descriptor release from transport/reader/actor completion. No source repair to the listener may be presented as causal closure of the HTTPS failures.

Required deterministic tests (new declarations, existing suite concurrency unchanged)
T1 Closed before accept: create listener, close twice, await real close completion, invoke accept on an existing off-pool worker, require nil and zero post-close accept syscalls. No client is needed.
T2 Close while a borrower is held before poll/accept: per-instance observation hook outside locks pauses the worker after acquisition. close must retire promptly and not call physical close; repeated close must be harmless. Release the barrier, require nil, one final close and completion notification. Do not use sleeps to create the ordering.
T3 Late successful accept: real loopback client reaches accept; pause after successful raw accept before publication. Close listener, resume, require no published connection, accepted descriptor closed exactly once and client EOF. Distinguish this from the existing admission22 test, which acts after acceptConnection has already returned.
T4 Stale readiness/EINTR: a bounded per-instance syscall seam returns readiness then EAGAIN, or EINTR at poll/accept, with close ordered before retry. Verify retirement is checked, no blocking accept, no spin/unbounded retry and no final-close race. Inject setup failures at listener and accepted F_GETFL/F_SETFL; verify exclusive-owner cleanup and no result leak.
T5 Deterministic descriptor-number reuse: use a tiny per-instance fake descriptor table/syscall seam that makes the retired number available to an unrelated sentinel only upon physical close. Hold a real ownership borrow, order close, and prove the table cannot reuse the slot yet; release, reuse the exact number, then invoke duplicate close/retry/timeout-setter paths and prove none targets the sentinel. Also test control-borrow shutdown finishing after the I/O borrower. This is a deterministic lifetime-model test, labelled as such. Do not use dup2 to overwrite a still-borrowed real fd, which would invalidate the ownership contract and risk unrelated parallel tests; do not call a stress loop until one reuse happens a deterministic test.
T6 Connection paths: separately pause a borrow before send, each recv family and each timeout setter; retire and release in controlled order, require no premature physical close and no post-release syscall. Include read+write concurrent borrowers and last-release ordering. Exercise success, EOF, short reads/writes, EINTR, error and cancellation defers. Test two simultaneous close callers and multiple async completion waiters, including waiter registration after completion.
T7 Native connected cancellation: on dedicated workers, block a real receive and a deliberately backpressured send, then cancel with the existing token/close mechanism. Require the actual worker completion and once-only close without relaxed production deadlines. Keep finite independent test guards to fail a hang; a timeout guard is not a passing cancellation. Confirm accepted sockets are blocking via flags and positive receive/send timeouts are retained; zero means no incidental idle expiry.
T8 Server/transport integration: stopped server listener can be rebound after stop's close completion; stop before accept worker starts; stopped pool rejects an already-transferred late arrival; successful HTTP keep-alive and WebSocket upgrade still work; current transport close/cancellation/generation tests and listener-using admission22 regression continue unchanged. Preserve the invalid-Connection upgrade case that appeared in the sample. Do not assert that these passes alone explain the historical stall.

Per-instance hooks must be nil/default in production, invoked outside ownership locks, and unable to replace the real completion signal. The syscall seam should be narrow enough to verify the ownership machinery rather than duplicate its implementation; combine its deterministic ordering tests with actual socket success/cancellation tests. Tests must never force-close/reassign an fd still owned by the implementation. No global hook, reduced suite concurrency, larger original deadline or repeated retry-until-pass campaign.

Platform validation and documentation basis
Use poll rather than select/FD_SET to avoid a new FD_SETSIZE limit in descriptor-heavy tests. Check Darwin/Glibc imports and pollfd/events/nfds_t conversions at the existing Swift6.2 floor; use portable accept plus fcntl rather than assuming accept4 availability. Preserve Darwin SO_NOSIGPIPE/Linux MSG_NOSIGNAL, TCP_NODELAY and unchanged accepted timeout setup. No local Linux execution was performed or is proposed here; fresh hosted Linux6.2/6.4 and macOS qualification remains root-owned after source review. Focused ordinary/ASan/TSan tests then required final-source native/hosted gates must use a new frozen source; prior failed23/22 receipts remain unchanged.

The following primary interface documentation was read through the web tool. It supports API choices, not a claim about the sampled kernel's internal cause. Apple pages are archived and do not prove current-runtime latency:
- Apple accept(2): nonblocking listener returns when no connection is queued; accepted socket is a separate descriptor. https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/accept.2.html
- Apple poll(2): finite milliseconds wait, timeout result0, EINTR and POLLERR/HUP/NVAL. https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/poll.2.html
- Apple fcntl(2): F_GETFL/F_SETFL and O_NONBLOCK. https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/fcntl.2.html
- Apple shutdown(2): connected full-duplex shutdown; ENOTCONN is possible. It does not establish that listener accept is unblocked. https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/shutdown.2.html
- Apple close(2): removes a descriptor; documents possible close errors. https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/System/Conceptual/ManPages_iPhoneOS/man2/close.2.html
- Linux man-pages accept(2), the kernel/C-library interface documentation project: BSD/Linux flag inheritance differs; explicitly set accepted flags; readiness alone can go stale, so nonblocking accept remains necessary. https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/accept.2.html
- Linux man-pages close(2): closing while another thread uses the fd can race with reuse; blocked Linux I/O may retain the open-file description; retrying close can close a reused number. https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/close.2.html
- Linux man-pages poll(2): finite wait can overrun through scheduling, so no hard wall-clock guarantee. https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/poll.2.html
